1
00:00:01,600 --> 00:00:10,165
At 01:19 on 8 March 2014, a voice from the cockpit of Malaysia Airlines flight 370 spoke

2
00:00:10,165 --> 00:00:14,606
to air traffic control for the last time.

3
00:00:14,856 --> 00:00:18,476
“Good night. Malaysian three seven zero.”

4
00:00:19,026 --> 00:00:23,148
Minutes later, it vanished from air traffic control radar.

5
00:00:23,148 --> 00:00:28,573
Twelve years and three seabed searches later, it has never been found.

6
00:00:28,723 --> 00:00:32,341
This is the story of what we know, and what we still don’t.

7
00:00:34,933 --> 00:00:40,586
Flight 370 was the overnight service from Kuala Lumpur to Beijing.

8
00:00:40,586 --> 00:00:44,547
On board were 227 passengers and 12 crew.

9
00:00:44,597 --> 00:00:46,456
Most were Chinese citizens.

10
00:00:46,456 --> 00:00:53,308
Others came from Malaysia, Indonesia, Australia, India, France, the United States and beyond.

11
00:00:53,358 --> 00:00:58,183
Among them were a group of Chinese artists returning from an exhibition,

12
00:00:58,183 --> 00:01:02,000
20 employees of a chip company, and two infants.

13
00:01:03,200 --> 00:01:11,199
It took off at 00:42. The Boeing 777 was flown by Captain Zaharie Ahmad Shah,

14
00:01:11,199 --> 00:01:19,856
who had more than 18,000 flying hours, and First Officer Fariq Abdul Hamid, 27.

15
00:01:19,906 --> 00:01:26,442
The plan was routine: climb northeast over the South China Sea, hand over

16
00:01:26,442 --> 00:01:33,160
to Vietnamese controllers at waypoint IGARI, land in Beijing at 06:30.

17
00:01:33,210 --> 00:01:37,121
At 01:07 the aircraft sent an automatic maintenance report.

18
00:01:37,121 --> 00:01:40,306
It was the last one it would ever send.

19
00:01:41,300 --> 00:01:46,096
At 01:19, Kuala Lumpur told the crew to contact Ho Chi Minh City.

20
00:01:46,096 --> 00:01:48,907
The reply was calm and ordinary.

21
00:01:48,957 --> 00:01:51,280
The crew never made that call.

22
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:57,322
Less than two minutes later, near IGARI, its transponder stopped transmitting.

23
00:01:57,322 --> 00:02:01,102
Flight 370 disappeared from civilian radar.

24
00:02:01,252 --> 00:02:07,975
But military radar, which needs no transponder, was still watching.

25
00:02:07,975 --> 00:02:18,006
It saw an unidentified aircraft turn sharply back to the southwest, most likely flown by hand.

26
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:21,886
The aircraft crossed back over the Malay Peninsula,

27
00:02:21,886 --> 00:02:26,727
passed just south of Penang Island and flew out over the Strait of Malacca.

28
00:02:26,777 --> 00:02:33,160
No jets were sent to intercept it. Controllers in two countries traded calls for hours.

29
00:02:33,160 --> 00:02:37,131
The rescue centre was not activated until 05:30.

30
00:02:37,281 --> 00:02:44,057
At 02:22, about 200 nautical miles northwest of Penang, military radar lost it.

31
00:02:44,057 --> 00:02:47,660
No radar ever saw Flight 370 again.

32
00:02:48,867 --> 00:02:55,075
But the aircraft left one more trace. Three minutes later, its satellite terminal logged back

33
00:02:55,075 --> 00:02:59,263
on to an Inmarsat satellite high above the Indian Ocean.

34
00:02:59,313 --> 00:03:02,103
Every hour after that, a ground station

35
00:03:02,103 --> 00:03:07,256
in Perth exchanged a brief automatic signal with the plane: a handshake.

36
00:03:07,256 --> 00:03:10,425
It carried no position and no message.

37
00:03:10,475 --> 00:03:16,606
But the time each signal took told investigators how far the aircraft was from the satellite.

38
00:03:16,606 --> 00:03:20,483
Each handshake placed the plane somewhere on a ring.

39
00:03:20,533 --> 00:03:24,717
Tiny shifts in the signal’s frequency showed it was heading south,

40
00:03:24,717 --> 00:03:27,132
into the southern Indian Ocean.

41
00:03:27,182 --> 00:03:32,229
Twice, the airline called the aircraft by satellite phone. No one answered.

42
00:03:33,134 --> 00:03:38,076
At 06:30, when it should have been landing in Beijing, the plane was still flying.

43
00:03:38,126 --> 00:03:45,887
At 08:19 it sent one last, partial handshake: most likely the engines running out of fuel

44
00:03:45,887 --> 00:03:54,010
and a backup power unit briefly reviving the terminal. An hour later, it did not answer.

45
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:56,923
That final ring became known as the 7th arc.

46
00:03:56,923 --> 00:04:01,076
Somewhere close to it, the aircraft came down into the sea.

47
00:04:01,226 --> 00:04:06,023
On 24 March, Prime Minister Najib Razak announced that, according

48
00:04:06,023 --> 00:04:11,566
to the new data, Flight 370 had ended in the southern Indian Ocean.

49
00:04:12,567 --> 00:04:16,712
What followed became the most expensive search in aviation history.

50
00:04:16,762 --> 00:04:20,832
Ships and aircraft from many nations combed the ocean surface.

51
00:04:20,832 --> 00:04:26,806
In April, an Australian ship picked up pings that seemed to come from the flight recorders.

52
00:04:26,806 --> 00:04:28,372
They led nowhere.

53
00:04:28,422 --> 00:04:36,474
Then the hunt moved to the seabed: unmapped undersea mountains, water up to 6 km deep.

54
00:04:36,474 --> 00:04:41,606
Ships towing sonar searched more than 120,000 km².

55
00:04:41,656 --> 00:04:47,216
In January 2017 Malaysia, Australia and China suspended the search.

56
00:04:47,216 --> 00:04:55,099
Australia’s investigators called it “almost inconceivable” that a large airliner could still be

57
00:04:55,099 --> 00:04:56,213
missing.

58
00:04:56,363 --> 00:05:04,600
In 2018 Ocean Infinity tried again with robot submarines, on a “no find, no fee” basis.

59
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:11,259
In four months it covered another 112,000 km². It found nothing.

60
00:05:12,467 --> 00:05:15,737
The ocean, though, had begun to give something back.

61
00:05:15,787 --> 00:05:22,624
In July 2015, on Réunion Island, beach cleaners found a piece of wing covered in barnacles.

62
00:05:22,624 --> 00:05:28,709
It was a flaperon from Flight 370: the first proof it had ended in the sea.

63
00:05:28,759 --> 00:05:36,368
More pieces washed up in South Africa, Mozambique, Mauritius, Tanzania and Madagascar.

64
00:05:36,368 --> 00:05:43,365
Three are confirmed; many more are judged almost certain or highly likely.

65
00:05:43,415 --> 00:05:48,040
Drift models run backwards from the finds pointed to the 7th arc.

66
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:55,471
And the flaps appear to have been retracted, which argues against a controlled landing on water.

67
00:05:56,667 --> 00:06:01,293
In July 2018, Malaysian investigators published their final report.

68
00:06:01,293 --> 00:06:04,850
They could not say why the plane disappeared.

69
00:06:04,900 --> 00:06:08,615
The turns were most likely made under manual control.

70
00:06:08,615 --> 00:06:12,331
Nothing unusual was found in the pilots' backgrounds.

71
00:06:12,331 --> 00:06:16,147
Third-party interference could not be ruled out.

72
00:06:16,197 --> 00:06:21,482
They also found that air traffic control had failed to follow procedures that night.

73
00:06:22,467 --> 00:06:27,750
Without the wreckage, theories filled the silence. The evidence rules some of them out.

74
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:30,518
It did not crash in the South China Sea.

75
00:06:30,518 --> 00:06:35,927
Radar, satellite data and the debris show it flew for hours to the south.

76
00:06:35,977 --> 00:06:41,933
It did not land intact somewhere. The final signals point to a fast descent into the ocean.

77
00:06:41,983 --> 00:06:45,728
A deliberate act from the cockpit remains possible.

78
00:06:45,728 --> 00:06:52,337
A flight into the southern Indian Ocean on the captain’s home simulator fuelled suspicion,

79
00:06:52,337 --> 00:06:56,458
but investigators found no proof and named no one.

80
00:06:56,508 --> 00:07:02,569
A hijacking, a ghost flight after a fire, new methods that claim to pinpoint the wreck:

81
00:07:02,569 --> 00:07:04,633
none has been proven.

82
00:07:05,834 --> 00:07:09,899
In 2025 Ocean Infinity returned on the same terms:

83
00:07:09,899 --> 00:07:15,145
US$70 million if it finds the plane, nothing if it doesn’t.

84
00:07:15,195 --> 00:07:22,722
Across two short seasons, its robots surveyed about 7,500 km². Again, nothing.

85
00:07:22,772 --> 00:07:27,514
Malaysia has extended the contract to June 2027,

86
00:07:27,514 --> 00:07:32,706
to search the last 7,400 km² of the target area.

87
00:07:32,756 --> 00:07:36,757
The families are still waiting. Some have taken the airline to court.

88
00:07:36,757 --> 00:07:41,068
Many have spent twelve years asking governments to keep looking.

89
00:07:42,467 --> 00:07:46,500
Somewhere along a curved line in the southern Indian Ocean,

90
00:07:46,500 --> 00:07:52,691
several kilometres down, lies a Boeing 777 and the answer to what happened on board.

91
00:07:53,041 --> 00:07:57,511
Until it is found, 239 people remain missing.
